So puer aeternus, what does it mean? What is the definition? And it is a Latin term called the eternal boy. And there's a quote from Jung. The puer aeternus has all the qualities of the adolescent psychology, all that hopeful one-sidedness. That hopeful attempt.

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:36.409
The puer aeternus is nothing but life, life in blind sprouting forms, full of conflict, full of worry, full of nonsense. And you have the feelings of the extraordinary uprush of the life symbolized by the puer aeternus. So it is childlike, but already adolescent in the sense of adventurous, full of energy.

00:02:36.942 --> 00:02:44.491
Turning outward, but also being naive, stumbling, doing things maybe in a stupidly manner.

00:02:45.535 --> 00:03:04.469
And Jung sets this in the context of other big archetypes that we have as, for example, the wise old man or the, the earth mother or the shadow and the anima and the animus and all those figures that are represented in a certain way.

